/// Stop if a condition is false.
///
/// Checks a result and stops the script with `message` when the check fails: the error is
/// Z0001, the code of `error`, and the exit code is 1 (section 3.8 of the Language Reference).
/// When the check holds, nothing is printed and the script carries on, so an `assert` costs
/// nothing to read and guards a re-run: a row count after a filter, a share between 0 and 1, a
/// coefficient with the expected sign. `assert(equal(f(x), [1, 2]), "...")` is how a project
/// tests its own functions.
///
/// The condition is one Bool. `NA` is neither true nor false and is an error (Z0203), as it is
/// for `if`, so a check on a value that may be missing tests `is_missing` first; a column is not
/// one Bool either (Z0203): reduce it with `all` or `any`, as in `assert(all(d["x"] > 0), ...)`.
/// Like R's `stopifnot`, with a message of your own; like Stata's `assert`, which stops with
/// "assertion is false".
///
/// condition: The check, a Bool.
/// message: What to say when it fails.
/// returns: Nothing.
/// see also: error, warn, equal, all
/// > assert(1 + 1 == 2, "arithmetic still holds")
/// > let d = frame(x = [1, 2, 3])
/// > assert(d.n_rows() == 3, "three rows expected")
fn assert(condition: Bool, message: Str) -> Nothing {
    if !condition {
        error(message)
    }
}
